! Problem 2.1: Cyclic Bit Shifting (C22-6531 Programming Examples,
! p.12, Method 1). "Cyclic left shift a full word in DOG by 7 bit
! positions."
!
! The three instruction lines are copied verbatim from the manual. DOG
! itself is not shown defined in the excerpted problem (it is presumed
! to exist elsewhere in the full program); this file supplies it with
! a DD statement carrying a known test pattern so the assembled program
! is self-contained and independently checkable: rotate_left(DOG, 7).
! DOG is placed word-aligned (first in the file, so its bit address is
! 0) because the final ST(BU,64),DOG writes a 64-bit field, and this
! model's VFL store rejects any field that crosses a word boundary
! (see stretch/bsv/StretchVFL.bsv fieldCrosses / README "Toolchain")
! -- a DOG at any other alignment would trip that and silently no-op.
!
! DOG's top 7 bits (IBM bits 0-6) must be nonzero, or the middle
! +(BU,7),DOG instruction adds zero and the test would pass even if
! that instruction were broken -- verified by exercising all three
! instructions, not just L's own left-shift.

DOG     DD(BU,64,8), 18364758544493064720   !0xFEDCBA9876543210, top7=1111111

START   L(BU, 64-7), DOG+.7, 7    !leave room for DOG thru DOG +0.6
        +(BU, 7), DOG
        ST(BU,64), DOG
        BEW, $                    !halt (not in the manual excerpt --
                                   !added so the BSV runner has a stop
                                   !condition; see stretch/strap/README.md)
        END, START
